Chat Application

Overview

This project is a real-time chat application developed in Java using the client-server architecture. The server manages multiple client connections, allowing users to exchange messages seamlessly. Clients can connect to the server and interact with each other in real time.
Features
Server
Listens for client connections on a specified port.
Supports multiple simultaneous client connections.
Broadcasts received messages to all connected users.
Manages client connections and disconnections efficiently.

Client
Connects to the server to join the chat.
Sends messages and receives responses from other users.
Displays incoming messages from other users.
Prompts the user to enter a name before joining the chat.

Requirements
Java Development Kit (JDK 22 or later)
Command-line interface (CLI) to run the application

How to Run the Application

1 Running the Server
The server should be started first, allowing it to accept client connections. Upon launching, it displays a message indicating that it is running and waiting for client connections.

2 Running Clients
Multiple clients can be started simultaneously. Each client is required to enter a username upon launch and can then send and receive messages from other connected users.

3 Testing Communication
Sending Messages: Users can type and send messages, which will be displayed to all connected users in the chat.
Handling Duplicate Messages: The server ensures that duplicate messages are processed correctly.
Client Disconnection: If a client disconnects, the remaining users should continue communicating without issues, and the server should handle disconnections efficiently.

Implementation Details

Server
Responsible for handling client connections and managing communication.
Includes essential components such as connection management, user name storage, and message routing.
Receives messages from clients and broadcasts them to all connected users.

Client
Allows users to connect to the server and participate in the chat.
Requires users to enter a name before joining the chat.
Manages both outgoing and incoming messages efficiently.
Uses multithreading to receive messages from the server while allowing the user to type responses.

Troubleshooting
Connection Issues: Ensure the server is running and that the client is connecting to the correct server address and port.
Messages Not Appearing: Verify that messages are being sent correctly and that the server is broadcasting them properly.
Handling Client Disconnections: Ensure the server correctly handles user disconnections without disrupting the chat session.

